      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the relationship given: \\(\\alpha\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\beta\\). This means that there exists a constant \\(k\\) such that:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\beta = k\n\\]\n\nWe are given that \\(\\alpha = 4\\) when \\(\\beta = 9\\). We can use this information to find the constant \\(k\\). Substituting the given values into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 9 = k\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we find:\n\n\\[\nk = 36\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the constant \\(k\\), we can use it to find \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\). Using the equation \\(\\alpha \\beta = k\\), we substitute \\(\\beta = -72\\) and \\(k = 36\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\alpha\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
