      "text": "Since $\\alpha$ is inversely proportional to $\\beta$, we can write the relationship as $\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = k$ for some constant $k$.  To find $k$, we can use the given information that $\\alpha = 4$ when $\\beta = 9$:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 9 = k \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k = 36\n\\]\n\nNow, we want to find $\\alpha$ when $\\beta = -72$. We can use the value of $k$ we found and the new value of $\\beta$ to find $\\alpha$:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, when $\\beta = -72$, $\\alpha = \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}$.\nTo solve the problem, we start by using the fact that \\(\\alpha\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\beta\\). This means that \\(\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = k\\) for some constant \\(k\\). We are given that \\(\\alpha = 4\\) when \\(\\beta = 9\\). We can use this information to find the value of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = 4 \\cdot 9 = 36 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k = 36\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find the value of \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\). We use the relationship \\(\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = k\\) again, but this time with \\(\\beta = -72\\) and \\(k = 36\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, when \\(\\beta = -72\\), \\(\\alpha = \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\).",
